# Database (Postgres — e.g. Vercel Postgres, Neon, or Supabase connection string)
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@host:5432/dbname"

# Google OAuth (create credentials in Google Cloud Console -> APIs & Services -> Credentials)
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# Auth.js
AUTH_SECRET="" # generate with: npx auth secret
NEXTAUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Vercel Blob (Storage tab -> Create Database -> Blob in your Vercel project;
# auto-populated in Vercel deployments, only needed manually for local dev)
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=""







Here's everything you need, going forward:

Start the app
npm run dev
Then open http://localhost:3000

View/edit your database
npm run db:studio
Then open http://localhost:5555

Other useful commands
npm install          # after pulling new dependencies
npm run db:push      # after any prisma/schema.prisma changes
npm run db:seed      # re-run seeding (e.g. to promote a new SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL)
npm run build        # production build
npm run start        # run the production build (after npm run build)

Notes for next time you start the project:
- .env is already fully configured (Google OAuth, AUTH_SECRET, database URL) — nothing more to do there.
- The database file already exists at prisma\dev.db with your data — no need to re-run db:push/db:seed unless you change the schema or want to reseed.
- To stop the dev server, just press Ctrl+C in that terminal (or close the terminal window).
- If port 3000 is already in use, Next.js will usually auto-pick another port (3001, etc.) and print it in the terminal output.

That's really it — npm run dev is the one command you'll use almost every tim
